== Public concept versus BITwiki application audit ==

Wave 1 deliberately prevents BITwiki's project vocabulary from becoming universal encyclopedia definitions.

Public concepts:

<pre>
Evidence
Uncertainty
Epistemic status
Provenance
Validation
</pre>

Project applications:

<pre>
BITwiki:Evidence
BITwiki:Uncertainty
BITwiki:Epistemic status
BITwiki:Provenance
BITwiki:Claim validation
</pre>

The public pages define the broader concept first. Project-specific taxonomies appear only in explicitly labeled BITwiki application sections.

This is especially important for [[Epistemic status]]: the public article describes a family of epistemically evaluative properties rather than presenting BITwiki's six-state controlled vocabulary as a universal philosophical ladder.

== Terminology-variation audit ==

=== Reproducibility / replication ===

The Core pages use the National Academies 2019 convention as a clearly labeled convention:

<pre>
reproducibility → same data / same computational analysis
replication     → new data / same scientific question
</pre>

The pages explicitly state that terminology differs across fields.

The metrology meaning of reproducibility is separately identified under [[Measurement]] and [[Accuracy and precision]].

=== Verification / validation ===

The Core definitions remain broad.

Engineering-specific "built right?" / "right thing built?" shorthand is presented as an application, not the universal definition.

Model verification, formal/software verification, source verification, engineering validation, construct validity, predictive validation, and data validation remain distinguishable.

=== Accuracy / precision / uncertainty ===

The articles preserve VIM distinctions:

* accuracy is not precision;
* accuracy is qualitative under VIM3 rather than a generic numerical score;
* trueness and bias relate to systematic effects;
* precision concerns replicate agreement under stated conditions;
* measurement uncertainty is not identical to measurement error or generic "accuracy".

